…has reached its limit. Jun Chen, strive diligently at this! Nevertheless, the people of Yin cherished the virtue of the Duke of Zhou. Jun Chen and Bi Gong succeeded him; as the times changed and customs shifted, there should have been no further cause for concern. Yet their habit of wavering in allegiance may not have changed. Could this mean that Zhou’s virtue was still deficient? It means only that they had not forgotten the virtue of Yin’s former kings. Alas! The people of Zhou could ensure that the people of Yin did not forget Zhou, yet could not ensure that they did not forget Yin [?]. From this I know that the virtue of Yin and Zhou reached the highest degree of flourishing.

Classic of Poetry

In the south are trees with downward-curving branches;
Kudzu and other creepers entwine them.
Joyful is the noble one;
Blessings bring her peace.
In the south are trees with downward-curving branches;
Kudzu and other creepers cover them.
Joyful is the noble one;
Blessings sustain her.
In the south are trees with downward-curving branches;
Kudzu and other creepers wind around them.
Joyful is the noble one;
Blessings complete her happiness.

Xie Yi

Comment by Associate Examiner Chen, Instructor: The word jiu in the commentary has the same meaning as in the Rites of Zhou, where the coloring of gui tablets and bi disks is described as having three or two stages of application. Its meaning is akin to “entwine,” rather than “make complete.” I record this to resolve the doubt.

Comment by Associate Examiner Wu, Instructor: In describing the extension of kindness to those below as “joyful,” the poet shows skill in expressing meaning. This essay’s language and ideas blend seamlessly, without displaying their artifice: its author is an accomplished writer.

Comment by Examiner Xiao, Instructor: Clear and precise.

Comment by Examiner Pan, Professor: Gentle and generous in tone.

