“What emperors and kings seek is good government;
Diligent learning must come first.
Below, one acts as master of the people;
Above, one receives Heaven’s charge.
To achieve good government, a foundation is essential;
When the foundation is correct, transformation naturally brings peace.
In what the ruler studies,
There is an order of precedence.
Rectify the mind and make the intentions sincere;
Once the resolve is settled, it will surely not shift.
Once my resolve can be settled,
How could the way of order again be overturned?
Cultivating the person rests upon rectifying the mind;
Ordering the family and state follows the same principle.
When the state is ordered, all becomes bright and clear;
The myriad lands then live in harmony.
‘Ah, the transformation!’ says the Canon of Emperor Yao;
‘Think of emulating’ appears in the ode to King Wen.
All transformation begins with cultivating oneself;
My thoughts are now earnestly devoted to this.”
He then personally wrote out his imperial calligraphy and bestowed it upon his assisting ministers, commanding them to compose matching poems. Among their verses were these:
“Without desires, virtue becomes luminous;
By making stillness central, resolve does not shift.
The single principle unfolds into myriad distinctions;
Scrutiny perceives even the tips of the finest hairs.”
